  <subsection>
    <title>Mechanical Vibrations</title>

    <!-- TODO figure -->
    <p>
      Consider a mass attached to a spring, displaced from its resting position by <m>x(t)</m> at time <m>t</m>.
      The force acting on the mass is modeled by the differential equation:
      <md>
        <mrow> mx'' \amp = -c x' - kx +F(t). </mrow>
      </md>
      The constant <m>m</m> is the mass of the object.
      The term <m>-cx'</m> represents the contribution of friction, with a nonnegative constant <m>c</m>.
      The term <m>-kx</m>, with positive constant <m>k</m>, comes from Hooke's Law.
      The term <m>F(t)</m> represents an external force that may be applied.
      The system is called <term>forced</term> if <m>F(t)</m> is not identically zero.
      Otherwise, the system is called <term>unforced</term>.
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Free undapmed motion</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>F(t) = 0, c = 0</m>.
          Then:
          <md>
            <mrow> mx'' + kx \amp = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow> x'' + \frac{k}{m} x \amp = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow> x'' + \omega_0^2 x \amp = 0, \quad \omega_0 = \sqrt{\frac{k}{m}} </mrow>
          </md>
          The roots of the characteristic polynomial are <m>\pm i \omega_0</m>, so the general solution is:
          <md>
            <mrow> x(t) \amp = A \cos(\omega_0 t) + B\sin(\omega_0 t) </mrow>
          </md>
          After applying trig identities, this can be rewritten:
          <md>
            <mrow> x(t) \amp = C \so(\omega_0 t - \gamma), </mrow>
          </md>
          where <m>C</m> is the <term>amplitude</term> of the wave, <m>\omega_0</m> is the <term>angular frequency</term>, and <m>\gamma</m> is the <term>phase shift</term>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>